{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact clean head d99b80a0cb85f79546bd91a1b19701973b94402a, judging artifact 665306e67db69bbbc36d976def49638f15b7f282. One finding, in the area you asked me to attack.\n\nAlias deduplication and ordering are locale-sensitive, so two readers of the same log can see different alias sets. `distinctLatestLabels` keys its map on `label.value.toLocaleLowerCase()` and then sorts with `a.value.localeCompare(b.value)`, both without an explicit locale, so both follow whatever locale the reader's runtime is in.\n\nMeasured rather than reasoned. In a Turkish locale `\"API\".toLocaleLowerCase()` is `\"apı\"` with a dotless i, which is not equal to `\"api\"` — so a topic carrying both `API` and `api` collapses to one alias for most readers and stays two for a Turkish one. Ordering diverges more widely: sorting `[\"Ångström\",\"Zebra\",\"apple\"]` gives `Ångström,apple,Zebra` under `en` and `apple,Zebra,Ångström` under `sv`.\n\nThat contradicts the condition as written — alias deduplication deterministic — and it contradicts something larger. This project's claim is that a position in the record means one thing for every reader. An alias list whose contents depend on the reader's locale is a small hole in that, but it is a hole in exactly the property the system sells, and it is the kind that never shows up locally because everyone developing it shares a locale.\n\nThe fix is small and I would rather name it than leave it open: use `toLowerCase()` for the dedup key and a codepoint comparison, or pass an explicit locale to both. Either makes the choice a function of the log. If you deliberately want reader-local collation for display, that is defensible, but then the dedup key must still be locale-independent, because dedup changes what exists rather than how it is shown.\n\nEverything else you asked about holds, and I checked each rather than assuming.\n\nRoot text is not rewritten and ancestry is not distorted. `root` stays the signed statement, `title` is `titleLabel?.value ?? topicTitle(root.text)` — a display fallback, not a mutation — and grouping still comes from `topicOf(statement.event)`, which labels do not touch. The label is additive over the structure rather than a transformation of it, which is the distinction that matters: an edit wearing a view's clothes would be unreadable as either.\n\nLatest-live title selection is deterministic. `latestLabel` reduces on `order` from `orderByEvent`, and `distinctLatestLabels` sorts explicitly rather than relying on map order. JS Maps are insertion-ordered, so the randomised-iteration failure I went looking for does not exist here.\n\nRetirement and staleness are handled with more care than the condition required. A retired or stale label statement is skipped from search entirely, not merely dropped from the alias list, so repeating a retired alias in explanatory prose cannot keep it accidentally searchable — and the label fields are filtered out of the generic body sweep so a value only enters search while its label is live.\n\nThe generated UI matches its source: `index.html` references `index-CxxDju6e.js`, that bundle is the only one in assets, and it contains the new fields. `npm test` passes 69 tests.\n\nOne thing that is not a finding but reads as one. The comment says \"One statement carries one field so its authorship and retirement remain independently visible\", and the code accepts a statement carrying both — recording a title and an alias from it. The behaviour is coherent, but the comment states as a rule something that is a convention, and the dual-field case you flagged is exactly where a reader would go looking for the rule to be enforced.\n\nConfirmed by execution: the locale behaviour above, `npm test`, the bundle contents, and the clean worktree at this head. Confirmed by reading only: the retirement and search-exclusion paths.","body":{"head":"d99b80a0cb85f79546bd91a1b19701973b94402a","verdict":"changes-requested"}}